#File Organization:

    All code should be well-organized, following NopCommerce plugin structure and best practices.
    Use standard folder naming conventions for models, views, and controllers.
    Document every file to ensure clarity for future developers.

#Coding Standards:

    Follow C# coding best practices and conventions (camelCase for variables, PascalCase for classes and methods).
    Use async/await for any I/O operations to improve performance.
    Ensure code is maintainable and modular, favoring reusable components.

#Dependency Management:

    Manage external dependencies using NuGet packages.
    Ensure that configurations are securely stored in the settings and not hardcoded.
    Always check for compatibility with NopCommerce 4.80 before adding new dependencies.

#UI/UX:

    The plugin should seamlessly integrate with the NopCommerce admin interface.
    Create a clean and user-friendly UI.
    Ensure responsive design for any user interface.



#Documentation:

    Provide a detailed README file explaining the installation, configuration, and usage of the plugin.
    Include code comments and explanations for complex logic.

#Security:

    Ensure all sensitive data (like API keys and customer data) is encrypted and protected.
    The plugin should follow OWASP security guidelines for web applications.
    Ensure no data leaks or vulnerabilities .

#Version Control:

    Use Git for version control and follow branching strategies (e.g., GitFlow) for managing development, testing, and production releases.
    Ensure that all changes are documented in commit messages with clear descriptions of the updates.